= Benchmark contamination — Simple =

'''Benchmark contamination''' happens when a model has already been exposed to benchmark questions, answers, or very similar material during training or tuning.

The benchmark may then look like a test of new ability even though some of the material is no longer new.

Contamination can be exact copies, near-copies, answer explanations, or repeated tuning against the benchmark.

A contaminated benchmark is not automatically completely useless, but its score is weaker evidence of independent generalization.

